{
  "dom_id": "step-q11725",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Which of the matrices below does **not** have an inverse?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 8 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "- The matrix $A$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix. So, it is not invertible.\n\n- For the matrix $B$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(B)=6\\cdot 4-8\\cdot 3=0.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(B)=0,$ we conclude that $B$ is not invertible.\n\n- For the matrix $C$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(C)=(-1)\\cdot 5-1\\cdot 5=-10.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(C)\\ne 0,$ we conclude that $C$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, only $A$ and $B$ do not have an inverse.",
